WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.347
Some changes make a new
material. Some changes do not.

2
00:00:08.567 --> 00:00:12.004
Ask one question: can you
get the first material back?

3
00:00:12.504 --> 00:00:16.730
Ice is a solid. Heat it and
it melts into water, a liquid.

4
00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:19.876
Put the water in the
freezer, and it is ice again.

5
00:00:20.096 --> 00:00:25.018
Melting and freezing keep the same
material. It is water all along.

6
00:00:25.238 --> 00:00:28.907
Chocolate melts in the sun. In
the fridge it sets hard again.

7
00:00:29.127 --> 00:00:33.167
It looks different, but it is still
chocolate. You can get it back.

8
00:00:33.667 --> 00:00:37.940
Here is a raw egg in a pan. The
egg white is runny and clear.

9
00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:43.268
Predict first. When the cooked
egg cools, will it go back to raw?

10
00:00:43.488 --> 00:00:48.364
Now heat it, three times. The clear
part turns white and goes solid.

11
00:00:48.584 --> 00:00:51.464
Now cool it down, and
look. It stays cooked.

12
00:00:51.684 --> 00:00:54.842
Say what happened, and whether
it matched your prediction.

13
00:00:55.062 --> 00:00:58.777
Cooking made a new material.
You cannot get the egg back.

14
00:00:59.277 --> 00:01:03.131
Wood burns in a fire. What
is left is ash and smoke.

15
00:01:03.351 --> 00:01:06.974
Can you turn the ash back
into wood? No, you cannot.

16
00:01:07.194 --> 00:01:11.187
A candle burns, too. It gets
shorter as the wax burns away.

17
00:01:11.407 --> 00:01:15.169
The wax turns into new materials
that go off into the air.

18
00:01:15.389 --> 00:01:19.011
Burning always makes a new
material. It cannot come back.

19
00:01:19.511 --> 00:01:22.669
Soft dough goes into the
oven, and bread comes out.

20
00:01:22.889 --> 00:01:26.697
Bread is a new material. There
is no going back to dough.

21
00:01:26.917 --> 00:01:31.701
Cake mix does the same. Runny mix
goes in, and a cake comes out.

22
00:01:31.921 --> 00:01:35.218
A baked cake cannot go
back to being runny mix.

23
00:01:35.718 --> 00:01:39.526
Heat can hurt. A grown-up handles
the heating, and you watch.

24
00:01:39.746 --> 00:01:42.950
Leave hot things for a grown-up
to move, with oven gloves.

25
00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:46.932
Never touch a pan to test
if it is hot. Ask, or wait.

26
00:01:47.152 --> 00:01:50.171
Tie back long hair, and
roll up your sleeves.

27
00:01:50.391 --> 00:01:54.617
The steam stops, but the metal
stays hot. Wait for it to cool.

28
00:01:54.837 --> 00:01:58.691
Heat changes materials, and
heat rules keep hands safe.

29
00:01:59.191 --> 00:02:02.953
So now you know. Melting and
freezing keep the same material.

30
00:02:03.173 --> 00:02:07.399
Cooking, burning and baking make
a new material. No going back.

31
00:02:07.619 --> 00:02:10.962
Predict first, then test
it and say what happened.

32
00:02:11.182 --> 00:02:16.012
Keep safe with heat. And ask: can
you get the first material back?
